<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Crown Colony of North Borneo</h1><p class="st-meta">1946 CE – 1963 CE · capital Jesselton</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">British colony formed from the war-ravaged territory of the North Borneo Company, which became the Malaysian state of Sabah.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-north-borneo-company">British North Borneo Company</a>, unable to finance rebuilding after the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/japanese-occupation-of-british-borneo">Japanese occupation of British Borneo</a>, transferred <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/north-borneo">North Borneo</a> to the crown on 15 July 1946. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/crown-colony-of-labuan">Labuan</a> was added to the new colony. Because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandakan">Sandakan</a> had been destroyed, the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-kinabalu">Jesselton</a>, today Kota Kinabalu.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A British governor ruled with advisory and legislative councils. The colonial government rebuilt towns and ports and expanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and copra exports, while education and health services grew slowly. Local political leaders emerged in the early 1960s, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuad-stephens">Donald Stephens</a> of the Kadazan community and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustapha-harun">Mustapha Harun</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/north-borneo">North Borneo</a> became self-governing in August 1963 and joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia</a> on 16 September 1963 as the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a>, despite a Philippine claim based on the old rights of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate</a>, a dispute that persists.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/crown-colony-of-north-borneo.jpg" alt="Crown Colony of North Borneo" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan This W3C-unspecified vector image was created with Inkscape . · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Borneo_(1948%E2%80%931963).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1946 CE – 1963 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jesselton</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William Goode, Donald Stephens</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Sunni Islam, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay, English, Kadazan-Dusun</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony of North Borneo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1954&amp;focus=crown-colony-of-north-borneo" aria-label="Crown Colony of North Borneo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7896 1954 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Crown Colony of North Borneo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8216 2210L8273 2215M8216 2210L4997 651M8216 2210L7823 2316"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8273 2215h0M4997 651h0M7823 2316h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8216 2210h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8216 2210h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1954&amp;focus=crown-colony-of-north-borneo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
